  "prompt": "Terse adversarial review. Offband MeshCore firmware. #453: make companion frame builders\nrespect the BLE ATT payload limit via a transport-provided maxFrameSize(), replacing\nhardcoded MAX_FRAME_SIZE-N caps. Background: a BLE notification carries only negotiated\nMTU-3 bytes; a larger frame is silently clipped (dropped tail) -- caused caplog near-full\ndata loss (#450, hardcoded 170 workaround now retired here). MTU varies (a T1000E link\nnegotiated 169-payload, not 176), so the cap must derive from the live negotiated MTU.\n\nDesign:\n- BaseSerialInterface: virtual size_t maxFrameSize() { return MAX_FRAME_SIZE; } (serial/TCP).\n- ESP32 SerialBLEInterface: cache _att_mtu (default 23) from onConnect(getPeerMTU) +\n  onMTUChange; maxFrameSize() = min(_att_mtu, MAX_FRAME_SIZE) - 3.\n- nRF52 SerialBLEInterface: maxFrameSize() queries Bluefruit.Connection(_conn_handle)->getMtu()\n  live (fallback 23 when not connected); = min(mtu, MAX_FRAME_SIZE) - 3.\n- Builders cap payload at _serial->maxFrameSize() - <header bytes>: caplog(-2), GPS(-2),\n  config scalar(-3), config stream drain(-3 and -4).\n\nReview concretely (cite file:line, rank severity):\n1. Correctness of both maxFrameSize() overrides: the -3 ATT-header math, the min() vs\n   MAX_FRAME_SIZE clamp, and the not-connected/pre-negotiation fallback (23-3=20). Any\n   unsigned-underflow if MTU<=3 or a builder subtracts its header from a tiny cap?\n2. ESP32 MTU caching: is onConnect's getPeerMTU + onMTUChange sufficient to have a correct\n   _att_mtu by the time a download drain runs? Any stale-across-reconnect issue (does it\n   reset on disconnect)? Thread/callback safety of the uint16_t read in maxFrameSize()?\n3. nRF52 querying getMtu() live each call: is Bluefruit.Connection(handle) safe to call\n   from the drain context, and correct when handle is BLE_CONN_HANDLE_INVALID?\n4. Builder caps: are the header subtractions correct for each frame layout, and does any\n   cap now risk underflow or a zero-length snapshot that changes drain termination?\n5. Did I miss a BLE-reachable variable frame builder that still uses MAX_FRAME_SIZE-N\n   (export-contact out_len, text-message clamps, device-info)? Which of those genuinely\n   need the same treatment vs are bounded < the BLE min anyway?\n6. Anything else -- API misuse (getPeerMTU/getMtu semantics), serial/TCP regression\n   (maxFrameSize default unchanged = MAX_FRAME_SIZE), behavior when MTU never negotiates.\nIf sound, say so.\n\n--- DIFF ---\ndiff --git a/examples/companion_radio/MyMesh.cpp b/examples/companion_radio/MyMesh.cpp\nindex e69f6ad7..425eea30 100644\n--- a/examples/companion_radio/MyMesh.cpp\n+++ b/examples/companion_radio/MyMesh.cpp\n@@ -1297,7 +1297,7 @@ void MyMesh::writeOffbandConfigScalar(uint8_t sub, const char* text) {\n   out_frame[0] = offband::RESP_CODE_OFFBAND_CONFIG;\n   out_frame[1] = sub;\n   size_t n = strlen(text);\n-  const size_t cap = MAX_FRAME_SIZE - 3;   // [0],[1] header + trailing NUL\n+  const size_t cap = _serial->maxFrameSize() - 3;   // [0],[1] header + trailing NUL (#453: transport/BLE-aware)\n   if (n > cap) n = cap;\n   memcpy(&out_frame[2], text, n);\n   out_frame[2 + n] = 0;\n@@ -1389,7 +1389,7 @@ void MyMesh::offbandStreamDrain() {\n       _ob_stream = OB_STREAM_NONE;\n       return;\n     }\n-    const size_t cap = MAX_FRAME_SIZE - 3;\n+    const size_t cap = _serial->maxFrameSize() - 3;   // #453: transport/BLE-aware\n     size_t remaining = strlen(&_ob_buf[_ob_off]);\n     size_t take = remaining < cap ? remaining : cap;\n     out_frame[0] = offband::RESP_CODE_OFFBAND_CONFIG;\n@@ -1428,7 +1428,7 @@ void MyMesh::offbandStreamDrain() {\n     char* line = &_ob_buf[_ob_off];\n     char* nl = strchr(line, '\\n');\n     size_t linelen = nl ? (size_t)(nl - line) : strlen(line);\n-    const size_t cap = MAX_FRAME_SIZE - 4;           // [0],[1],[2]=slot + trailing NUL\n+    const size_t cap = _serial->maxFrameSize() - 4;           // [0],[1],[2]=slot + trailing NUL (#453: transport/BLE-aware)\n     if (linelen > cap) linelen = cap;\n     if (linelen > 0) {\n       out_frame[0] = offband::RESP_CODE_OFFBAND_CONFIG;\n@@ -1458,7 +1458,7 @@ void MyMesh::handleCmdFrame(size_t len) {\n   if (cmd_frame[0] == CMD_OFFBAND_GPS) {\n     out_frame[0] = RESP_CODE_OFFBAND_GPS;\n     char* txt = (char*)&out_frame[1];\n-    const size_t cap = (size_t)MAX_FRAME_SIZE - 2;   // [0] header + trailing NUL\n+    const size_t cap = _serial->maxFrameSize() - 2;   // [0] header + trailing NUL (#453: transport/BLE-aware)\n     int n = snprintf(txt, cap, \"enabled=%d \", (int)_prefs.gps_enabled);\n     if (n < 0 || (size_t)n >= cap) n = 0;\n     sensors.getGpsStatusText(txt + n, cap - (size_t)n);\n@@ -2768,14 +2768,12 @@ void MyMesh::blockListDrain() {\n // being flooded. The ring is frozen (capture auto-stopped at START), so the\n // offset stream is stable. Snapshot returning 0 signals the buffer is exhausted.\n void MyMesh::caplogDrain() {\n-  // #450: cap CHUNK data so the frame fits ONE BLE notification. A full\n-  // MAX_FRAME_SIZE (176) frame does NOT fit BLE: the device negotiates MTU 176, so an\n-  // ATT notification carries only MTU-3 = 173 bytes -- a 176-B frame is clipped to 173,\n-  // dropping 3 data bytes per full chunk (only bites near-full downloads over BLE;\n-  // serial/TCP have no ATT header). MAX_FRAME_SIZE-6 = 170 data (172-B frame) clears the\n-  // 173 BLE limit with margin and matches the '+4 for transport codes' headroom baked\n-  // into MAX_FRAME_SIZE. Fits BLE + TCP + serial.\n-  const size_t cap = (size_t)MAX_FRAME_SIZE - 6;   // [0]=code, [1]=sub, -4 BLE ATT/transport headroom\n+  // #453: cap CHUNK data to what the transport can deliver atomically, minus the 2\n+  // header bytes ([0]=code, [1]=sub-code). maxFrameSize() is MAX_FRAME_SIZE on serial/\n+  // TCP and the negotiated ATT MTU-3 on BLE, so a chunk never overflows a BLE\n+  // notification regardless of the negotiated MTU. Retires #450's hardcoded 170, which\n+  // only fit